  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 125 AM CST Sat Nov 28 2026\n\nSurface high pressure over the Gulf Coast has built northward  \nand has resulted in light southwesterly winds over the lower \nMissouri River Valley. Temperatures are largely in the low 40s \nas of 07Z and are expected to fall into the low-to-mid 30s this \nmorning. Troughing will begin to dig into the Rockies through \nthe day today, leading to low amplitude ridging over the local \narea. Mid-level height rises along with 850-hPa WAA will help \ntemperatures to rise into the low-to-mid 50s this afternoon.\n\nA trough is forecast to eject across the Rockies this afternoon, \ninciting surface cyclogenesis across the High Plains, in the \nvicinity of the OK Panhandle. This system then tracks to the \neast through the overnight period, passing through southern MO \nand northern AR. The cyclone will pull Gulf moisture northward, \nwith the warm conveyor belt wrapping around the north side of \nthe system. With a mid-level vort maxima passing overhead and \nample moisture, precipitation is likely (80-95%) across northern\nMO this evening through Sunday morning. With warm temperatures \nalready in place and the system ingesting warm Gulf air, \ntemperatures are expected to remain above freezing through\nSunday morning. Thus it is expected that the p-type will be \nrain for the entirety of this event.\n\nRain will begin to move into northwest MO during the late  \nafternoon hours, spreading southeastward through the evening. By\nmidnight, precipitation should be falling across the vast \nmajority of the CWA. Rain will exit the area to the southeast, \nwith precipitation chances beginning to decrease across \nnorthwest MO by sunrise. The precipitation should fully exit the\narea by noon, although low-level clouds will remain over the \narea into the evening hours. The heaviest precipitation is \nexpected across northern MO, where the warm conveyor belt wraps \naround the system and a deformation zone looks to set up. NBM \nprobabilities show a 70-90% chance of exceeding 0.5\" along and \nnorth of the Missouri River, while the LREF has a 50-70% chance.\nDespite these relatively high chances to exceed 0.5\" of rain, \nprobabilities of seeing over 1\" are quite low, with the NBM \nshowing a 10-30% chance along and north of US-36 and the LREF \nhaving a <10% chance.\n\nAs the system exits to the east, Sunday will remain chilly, with \nhighs in the low-to-mid 40s thanks to 850-hPa CAA on the \nbackside of the trough. High pressure will begin to build across\nthe High Plains, leading to a tight pressure gradient over the \nregion. This will lead to a gusty day with northwest winds of \n15-20 mph gusting to 30-35 mph. Winds will ease from west to \neast through the afternoon and evening as the low pressure \nsystem continues to advance eastward. By Monday, low amplitude \nridging will begin to build over the central CONUS with surface \nhigh pressure sliding just east of the area, bringing southerly \nwinds and 850-hPa WAA back to the region. This pattern will \nremain in place through at least mid- week, allowing \ntemperatures to climb into the low- to-mid 60s (15-20F above \nnormal) by Wednesday and Thursday.\n\nModels are in fairly good agreement that another trough will  \neject across the central CONUS towards the end of the work week,\nalthough discrepancies remain on the exact placement and timing\nof this system. This is currently the next best chance for \nprecipitation across northern MO, with chances sitting in the \n40-60% range for Thursday into Friday.",
